# Test: an export-direction family-filter (NoFlow, action remove) withholds an
#       ipv4/flow route from the peer that carries it, and that same peer still
#       receives the per-family End-of-RIB marker.
#
# VALIDATES: export-direction family-filter removes ipv4/flow from originated
#            routes (the FlowSpec-route-reflector use case), and the MP-family EoR
#            exemption keeps graceful-restart signalling.
# PREVENTS:  originated/injected routes bypassing the egress export-filter gate;
#            the per-family EoR being wrongly suppressed by the same gate.
#
# Both claims are answered by one predicate, writeUpdateGated
# (internal/component/bgp/reactor/session_write.go): an originated UPDATE is
# offered to the peer's export chain unless update.IsEndOfRIBAnyFamily()
# (internal/component/bgp/message/eor.go) says it is a marker. The filter's
# remove action strips the ipv4/flow MP attribute, and an UPDATE that carries
# nothing else is suppressed whole (handleFilterUpdate,
# internal/component/bgp/plugins/filter_family/handler.go).
#
# NON-VACUITY (ai/rules/interop-and-goal-validation.md). This file used to state
# both claims and prove neither: its route injections were cmd=api: lines inside
# the peer block, which ze-peer records as documentation and nobody executes, so
# no route was ever announced and its rejection could not fire. The design below
# makes each claim fail on its own:
#
#   TWO PEERS, ONE ANNOUNCE. The observer announces one ipv4/flow route to both
#   peers with one command. filtered-peer carries the export filter and
#   unfiltered-peer does not, so the SAME bytes from the SAME producer in the
#   SAME run must reach one and not the other. The needle 01180A0100 is the
#   FlowSpec destination component for 10.1.0.0/24 (type 1, length 24, three
#   prefix bytes). It is a strict PREFIX of the 01180A010003 this file forbade
#   before, so it matches everything that pattern matched and more: the
#   rejection got stricter, not looser. unfiltered-peer's expect rule asserts
#   the same needle arrives, so a run in which the route was never produced, or
#   in which the needle is wrong, fails there rather than passing
#   filtered-peer's rejection for the wrong reason.
#
#   LINGER, BECAUSE A SATISFIED PEER STOPS ASSERTING. filtered-peer's expect rule
#   is the End-of-RIB, which arrives BEFORE the route. Without option=linger the
#   peer would finish at that frame and read no further ((*Peer).completed,
#   internal/test/peer/reject.go), so the route could arrive one frame late and
#   the rejection would never see it. The linger loop re-checks every frame it
#   reads against the rejections, and a rejection it finds RETRACTS the success
#   token the peer already printed, by writing RejectionMarker on the peer's own
#   output ((*Peer).rejected, internal/test/peer/reject.go). The verdict reads
#   that retraction after the token and lets it win (failedCheckPeers,
#   internal/test/runner/peer_contract.go). Until 715a54fad it did not, so this
#   file's CLAIM 1 was vacuous: the rejection fired, was returned, and was then
#   discarded.
#
#   PROVEN BY MUTATION, 2026-08-29. handleFilterUpdate
#   (internal/component/bgp/plugins/filter_family/handler.go) was changed to
#   accept the matched ipv4/flow UPDATE instead of stripping its MP attribute.
#   filtered-peer then printed "successful", "lingering", the forbidden frame,
#   and "ZE-PEER-REJECTED", and the runner reported this test FAILED on
#   stdin=filtered. Reverting the mutation returned it to green. CLAIM 1 now
#   discriminates.
#
#   THE DAEMON'S OWN COUNTERS, AS THE SECOND HALF. The observer asserts
#   updates-sent minus eor-sent on each peer. Those counters are bumped from
#   onMessageReceived, which writeUpdateGated reaches only after the socket write
#   succeeds, so a suppressed UPDATE never counts.
#
# THE TWO ADDRESSES OF A SESSION ARE DISTINCT (ai/rules/testing.md). A route
# whose NEXT_HOP is the destination peer's own address is withheld by RFC 4271
# Section 5.1.3 (originatedNextHopIsPeerOwn, reactor/forward_next_hop.go), which
# would suppress the subject route for a reason this test does not name.

# The peer under the export filter. Ze dials it, so it listens on 127.0.0.1.
stdin=filtered:terminator=EOF_FILTERED
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=30s
# Hold the session open so the rejection below outlives the marker it follows.
option=linger:value=true
# CLAIM 2: the ipv4/flow End-of-RIB crosses the export gate. MP_UNREACH_NLRI
# (type 15) carrying AFI 1 SAFI 133 and no withdrawn NLRI, and nothing else.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF001E0200000007900F0003000185
# CLAIM 1: the FlowSpec route does not. 01180A0100 is the destination component
# for 10.1.0.0/24, which unfiltered-peer proves is on the wire in this run.
reject=bgp:conn=1:pattern=01180A0100
EOF_FILTERED

# The control peer. Same family, same announce, no export filter. Listens on
# 127.0.0.2.
stdin=unfiltered:terminator=EOF_UNFILTERED
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=30s
# Hold this session open too. Without it the peer closes the moment its second
# expect rule matches, and the observer then reads per-peer state from a daemon
# that is tearing a session down underneath it.
option=linger:value=true
# A distinct BGP Identifier. ze-peer defaults to ze's own identifier with the
# last octet incremented, so both peers would present 1.2.3.5 and the second
# OPEN in AS 1 is refused by the RFC 6286 Section 2.1 uniqueness check
# (routerIDClaims.claim, reactor/routerid_unique.go).
option=open:value=router-id:id=1.2.3.6
# The same marker, so the two peers' End-of-RIB results are comparable: a build
# that stops sending the marker altogether fails here as well as on the filtered
# peer, and the difference between the two files stays the filter.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF001E0200000007900F0003000185
# THE CONTROL: the announced route, on the wire, byte for byte the needle the
# filtered peer forbids.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:contains=01180A0100
EOF_UNFILTERED


stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	external export-flowspec {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/filter-family-export-flowspec"
		encoder json
	}
}
bgp {
	policy {
		family-filter NoFlow {
			family ipv4/flow
			action remove
		}
	}
	peer filtered-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.3
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 1
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/flow { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		filter {
			export [ bgp-filter-family:NoFlow ]
		}
		attach process export-flowspec {
			send [ update ]
		}
	}

	peer unfiltered-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.2
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.4
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 1
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/flow { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		attach process export-flowspec {
			send [ update ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=filtered
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze-peer --bind 127.0.0.2 --port $PORT:stdin=unfiltered
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze --plugin ze.bgp-filter-family --plugin ze.bgp-nlri-flowspec -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=30s
expect=exit:code=0
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
